==English==

===Etymology===
Compound of {{compound|en|red|neck}}. Compare {{m|en|rooinek}}. The term originally characterized farmers that had a red neck, caused by sunburn from long hours working in the fields.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛdnɛk/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-redneck.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang|usually|pejorative|offensive|ethnic slur}} A poor, [[rural]], usually [[white]], person from the [[Southern United States]] or parts of the [[Midwest]] and [[Northeast]], especially one whose beliefs are seen as [[unsophisticated]] and [[backward]]; sometimes with additional connotations of being [[bigoted]].
#: {{syn|en|bogan<q:Australia>|cracker|hick|hillbilly|peckerwood|white trash|Thesaurus:chav|Thesaurus:country bumpkin}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title=Fight the Power|artist=Public Enemy|album=Fear of a Black Planet|year=1989|passage=Sample a look back you look and find / Nothing but '''rednecks''' for 400 years if you check}}
# {{lb|en|historical|slang|US}} Any of the [[miner]]s who wore red [[bandana]]s for identification during the [[West Virginia]] mine war of 1921.<ref>{{cite-web |title=West Virginia Division of Culture and History |url=http://www.wvculture.org/hiStory/minewars.html |accessdate=30 March 2008 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130604201312/http://www.wvculture.org/history/minewars.html |archivedate=4 June 2013 }}</ref>
# {{lb|en|historical|slang|US}} A member of a certain [[Baltimore]] street gang, active in 1859.<ref>[https://www.newspapers.com/clip/6830706/red_necks_are_violent_political_people/ The New York Times (New York, New York), 3 November 1859, Thu • Page 4 ]</ref>
# {{lb|en|UK|archaic|19th and 20th centuries|religious slur}} A [[Roman Catholic]].<ref>[https://www.newspapers.com/clip/6830356/redneck_a_term_used_by_protestants_in/ The Raleigh Register, (Raleigh, North Carolina), 19 March 1841, Fri • Page 2 ]</ref>

